# Line endings are part of the contract here, not a preference.
#
# Tests read source files and compare them: `tests/reference/coverage.test.ts` finds the SET
# options this runtime handles by matching the text of `vm.rs`, and the samples test compares
# imported documents. A checkout with CRLF endings makes those regexes miss - quietly, so the
# coverage map came out *wrong* rather than failing - and it has happened twice, both times in a
# fresh worktree, because `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows converts on checkout.
#
# So: source is LF everywhere, whatever the machine is set to. Anything a test compares as bytes
# is binary and git must not touch it at all - the Visual FoxPro fixtures especially, which are
# DBF tables full of text and would be mangled by a wrong guess.

* -text

*.rs        text eol=lf
*.ts        text eol=lf
*.tsx       text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
*.cjs       text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.jsonc     text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.html      text eol=lf
*.css       text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf
*.lock      text eol=lf
.gitignore  text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# FoxPro source and the documents the designers write
